---
import { countFact } from '../../lib/quiz-count';
/**
 * /psalm/: A psalm for every situation. St Athanasius's guide, whole and open.
 *
 * The hub the 78 psalm pages hang from (their breadcrumbs lead here), and the page a searcher
 * lands on when they type "a psalm for" whatever they are going through. It lists every
 * situation the quiz draws from his Letter to Marcellinus, in the reader's words, in the
 * seven browsing groups the quiz page uses, each a way in to its psalm's page. These are the
 * 53 the quiz can name; the letter pairs about a hundred
 * psalms with a need in all, and the page says so rather than calling the 53 "every" one.
 *
 * What the page adds of its own is the frame: who wrote the letter and why it has been
 * trusted (copied in front of the Psalms in the Codex Alexandrinus, in the 400s), what the
 * letter does NOT name and what it gives instead (the quiz's own honest page, said once for
 * everyone), and the numbering. Every situation line, piece of advice and section number is
 * the data's, read against his Greek. The owner approved the page on 2026-09-23.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import SectionHead from '../../components/SectionHead.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import { whichPsalm } from '../../lib/quizzes/which-psalm';
import { READING, SITUATIONS, GROUPS, psalmLinkFor } from '../../lib/strategies/reading';
import { quizHref } from '../../lib/engine/registry';
import { artFor } from '../../lib/art';
import { PSALM_TOPICS } from '../../lib/seo';
import '../../styles/pages/psalm.css';

const quiz = whichPsalm;
const art = artFor(quiz.slug);
const psalmCount = quiz.outcomes.length;

/* "116:1-9" reads "116:1–9"; a whole psalm is its number. */
const alsoName = (x: number | string) => String(x).replace('-', '–');
const alsoHref = (x: number | string) => `/psalm/${parseInt(String(x), 10)}/`;

/* What the letter does not name, and what it gives for what those things do to a person:
   the options of the quiz's own "none of those" page, in the words a reader would use. */
const NONE_SAY: Record<string, string> = {
  P102: 'When it is grinding you down',
  P42S: 'When it weighs on you and will not let you settle',
  P118: 'When it is making you afraid',
  P143W: 'When you need to bring it to God and do not know how'
};
for (const [id] of READING.none.options) {
  if (!NONE_SAY[id]) throw new Error(`/psalm/: no line for the none option ${id}`);
}

const url = new URL('/psalm/', Astro.site).href;
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'CollectionPage',
  name: 'A psalm for every situation',
  url,
  isPartOf: { '@id': new URL('/#website', Astro.site).href },
  about: {
    '@type': 'CreativeWork',
    name: 'Letter to Marcellinus',
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Athanasius of Alexandria' }
  },
  mainEntity: {
    '@type': 'ItemList',
    numberOfItems: SITUATIONS.length,
    itemListElement: GROUPS.flatMap(g => g.ids).map((id, i) => {
      const l = psalmLinkFor(id);
      return {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: i + 1,
        url: new URL(l.href, Astro.site).href,
        name: `${l.label}: ${PSALM_TOPICS[id]}`
      };
    })
  }
};
const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'A psalm for every situation', url: '/psalm/' }
];
---
<Base
  title="A psalm for every situation"
  description={`In the 300s St Athanasius matched the Psalms to what people go through. Here are ${SITUATIONS.length} of his situations, with his advice and the whole psalm.`}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
  <Hero
    title="A psalm for every situation"
    blue="every"
    kind={art ? 'engraving' : 'night'}
    image={art?.src}
    imageAlt={art?.alt}
    imagePosition={art?.objectPosition}
    imageLift={art?.lift}
    lede={`In the 300s, St Athanasius wrote a letter about praying the Psalms. Here are ${SITUATIONS.length} of the situations he named, with the psalm he gave for each one.`}
    tiles={[
      { n: SITUATIONS.length, label: 'Situations' },
      { n: psalmCount, label: 'Psalms' }
    ]}
    long
  >
    <a class="btn" href={quizHref(quiz)} slot="actions">Find yours <Icon name="arrow" size={15} /></a>
  </Hero>

  <div class="ref ref--psalm">
    <SectionHead title="Where this comes from" kicker="The Letter to Marcellinus" />
    <div class="pg-intro">
      <p>
        St Athanasius was the bishop of Alexandria in the 300s. His friend Marcellinus was
        getting over an illness and spent the time reading the Psalms. Athanasius wrote him a
        letter about them. In it he passed on what a studious old man had once taught him.
      </p>
      <p>
        The old man's point was simple. You read the rest of the Bible as other people's words.
        You say the Psalms as your own. He called them a mirror that shows you what is going on
        in your own soul. Then he went through one situation in life after another and named
        the psalm to pray for each. He believed every part of human life is found in them.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Church has trusted the letter ever since. One of the oldest Bibles in the world, the
        Codex Alexandrinus from the 400s, places it right in front of the Book of Psalms.
      </p>
      <p>
        Below are 53 of his situations in plain words. Tap one to read its psalm in full, with
        his advice. The letter also names psalms for feasts, for certain
        days of the week and for teaching the faith.
      </p>
      <p class="pg-cta">
        <Icon name="arrow" size={14} />
        <span>Not sure which is yours? <a href={quizHref(quiz)}>A few honest taps will find it</a>.</span>
      </p>
      <p class="pg-cta">
        <Icon name="arrow" size={14} />
        <span>How he said to pray them: <a href="/articles/how-to-pray-the-psalms/">six lessons from the letter</a>.</span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <nav class="pg-toc" aria-label="The seven groups">
      {GROUPS.map(g => (
        <a href={`#${g.id}`}>{g.title}<span class="pg-toc-n">{g.ids.length}</span></a>
      ))}
    </nav>

    {GROUPS.map(g => (
      <section class="pg-group" id={g.id} aria-labelledby={`${g.id}-h`}>
        <h2 class="pg-gtitle" id={`${g.id}-h`}>{g.title}</h2>
        <ul class="pg-list">
          {g.ids.map(id => {
            const o = READING.outcomes[id]!;
            const l = psalmLinkFor(id);
            return (
              <li class="pg-item">
                <a class="pg-main" href={l.href}>
                  <span class="pg-num" aria-hidden="true">{l.short}</span>
                  <span class="pg-body">
                    <span class="pg-seen">{o.seen}</span>
                    <span class="pg-meta">{l.label}</span>
                  </span>
                  <Icon name="arrow" size={14} />
                </a>
                {o.also.length > 0 && (
                  <p class="pg-also">
                    He also named {o.also.map((p, i) => (
                      <>{i > 0 && (i === o.also.length - 1 ? ' and ' : ', ')}<a href={alsoHref(p)}>Psalm {alsoName(p)}</a></>
                    ))}.
                  </p>
                )}
              </li>
            );
          })}
        </ul>
      </section>
    ))}

    <section class="pg-none" aria-labelledby="pg-none-h">
      <h2 id="pg-none-h">What his letter doesn't name</h2>
      <p>
        The letter covers a great deal of life, but not all of it. Athanasius names no psalm
        for a death, for an illness itself, or for a hard decision, and we won't pretend he
        does. His friend was just getting over an illness when he wrote, and still the letter
        names nothing for sickness. What he does name is a psalm for what those things do to
        a person.
      </p>
      <ul class="pg-list pg-list--none">
        {READING.none.options.map(([id]) => {
          const l = psalmLinkFor(id);
          return (
            <li class="pg-item">
              <a class="pg-main" href={l.href}>
                <span class="pg-num" aria-hidden="true">{l.short}</span>
                <span class="pg-body"><span class="pg-seen">{NONE_SAY[id]}</span><span class="pg-meta">{l.label}</span></span>
                <Icon name="arrow" size={14} />
              </a>
            </li>
          );
        })}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <p class="ps-greek">
      Athanasius numbered the psalms from the Greek Bible, so from Psalm 10 to Psalm 147 his
      number usually runs one lower than an English Bible's: his 50 is our 51. Every number
      here is the English one, and each psalm's page gives his as well.
    </p>

    <div class="qcard-wrap">
      <a class={`qcard qcard--plain qcard--${quiz.slug}`} href={quizHref(quiz)}>
        <div class="qcard-body">
          <p class="qcard-kicker">Your turn</p>
          <h2 class="qcard-title">{quiz.title}</h2>
          <p class="qcard-say">{quiz.tagline}</p>
          <ul class="qcard-facts">
            <li><Icon name="clock" size={13} /> {quiz.minutes} min</li>
            <li>{countFact(quiz)}</li>
          </ul>
          <span class="qcard-go go-pill">Start <Icon name="arrow" size={16} /></span>
        </div>
      </a>
    </div>

    <p class="ps-credit">
      Bible text: Berean Standard Bible, public domain. Athanasius’s advice is paraphrased
      from his Greek (Migne, Patrologia Graeca 27).
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
